\section{Two Meanings of CIL}

The acronym CIL does not identify a single kind of portable intermediate language. In Necula, McPeak, Rahul, and Weimer's \textit{CIL: Intermediate Language and Tools for Analysis and Transformation of C Programs}, it stands for \emph{C Intermediate Language}. It is a normalized representation of C that remains close enough to source to emit C again. It makes implicit coercions explicit and separates effectful instructions from expressions. That normalization makes analyses simpler without pretending C has a single implementation-independent ABI. A transformation that relies on structure layout, for instance, must know which implementation's layout it preserves when it emits C.

The CIL paper's \texttt{StartOf} operation illustrates the boundary. It handles the implicit conversion of an array lvalue to the address of its first element without treating an array and a pointer as interchangeable internal types. A C emitter normally leaves this conversion implicit again, relying on the context in which it prints the array. In particular, it cannot print every occurrence of the converted value as a bare array under \texttt{sizeof}: the size of an array there is not the size of a pointer. The paper retains size and alignment queries in the IR rather than always folding them before a transformation can change types.

In Jennifer Hamilton's \textit{Language Integration in the Common Language Runtime}, CIL is instead the \emph{Common Intermediate Language}. In that system the shipped code is accompanied by metadata for a managed execution environment. The Common Type System (CTS) distinguishes the representations and types supported by the runtime, and the Common Language Specification (CLS) restricts what an exposed interface should use to be consumable across participating languages. It is therefore possible to emit legal internal code that is not a good public interface for another language. Gordon and Syme's model of a fragment of Microsoft IL explains one reason the type system is richer than a simple description of stack values: it must account for the distinction between boxed and unboxed values and for the lifetime of managed pointers.

\section{The JVM File Is a Consumer Contract}

Lindholm, Yellin, Bracha, Buckley, and Smith's \textit{Java Virtual Machine Specification} describes both the class-file format and the behavior of the abstract machine. A method has local-variable slots and an operand stack, and instructions refer symbolically to entities through the run-time constant pool. The machine also defines resolution, initialization, and normal and abrupt completion. A compiler for a non-Java language can emit class files, but its source-specific semantics may still require library routines, synthetic classes, or a particular representation of data. Those conventions belong to the language's runtime interface, not to the verifier's definition of a valid class file.

\section{Worked Example: One Join, Two Producers}

Consider this schematic JVM method returning an \texttt{int}, with an integer flag in local slot zero. Labels stand for bytecode offsets; class-file metadata is omitted.

\begin{verbatim}
  iload_0
  ifeq Else
  iconst_1
  goto Join
Else:
  iconst_2
Join:
  ireturn
\end{verbatim}

The conditional consumes the flag, so on both incoming paths to \texttt{Join} the operand stack has depth one and the top has verification type \texttt{int}. A verifier for modern class files uses the method's initial frame and the declared stack-map frames at the relevant targets to check the transfers. Replacing \texttt{iconst\_2} with \texttt{aconst\_null} gives the same stack height, but the reference value cannot satisfy the required integer frame and return. Depth alone is not enough. Internally, a JIT can decode the two pushes to SSA value definitions and the join to one block parameter or \(\phi\)-node; it need not use an actual hardware stack for these temporaries.

\section{Which Boundaries Survive Distribution?}

Public bytecode format, managed API, and native calling convention are different layers. The same class file can be distributed to JVMs on multiple architectures, but a call to a native library requires a compatible library and a platform-specific boundary. Likewise, the fact that a field's type verifies does not ensure that the field exists in the linked version of its defining component. A robust producer tests artifact decoding, link-time resolution, and behavior on separately selected platforms, not just accepted stack maps. The dossier's Markdown Chapter~42, \textit{Adaptive ABI Negotiation}, takes up the further problem of choosing concrete interface conventions when independently produced pieces meet. None of these obligations requires the verifier to know how the producer's optimizing IR was organized.
